# interplay_limits_ring_wraps: A token passed through the largest root FIFO (0x20000, a ring of 0x1fc00 bytes) and two children over scattered pages (child 1: 16 pages, child 2: 5, every ring page followed in RAM by a page of 0xff), ten rounds: each exec increments a counter (compute_inc) and copies it into log[k]. Before each exec, a NOP whose header's length carries the ring to a chosen offset (only its header written; its payload is the ring's stale bytes), so that across the rounds each FIFO's exec header straddles the ring's end (6 bytes before it), an exec's payload straddles it, and on the children an exec's header straddles a page boundary (5 bytes before it) and a payload another; each FIFO laps its ring five times. Every command waits for the one before it (the NOP on the previous FIFO's exec, the exec on its own NOP), so the 60 interrupts come in token order, and log[k] = k + 1. Each round is rung once, as a guest must: a doorbell covering more than a ring's worth would overwrite commands not yet read.
